;;; A keyword pair inside a pattern is dropped in silence — M1f **W32**,
;;; register row `W32/kwpair-in-pattern` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked
;;; by S1f.5.0 (2026-11-08) from S1f.5.11's case (the inventory of
;;; 2026-11-05's G-15; its probe `kwpair_in_pattern.ein` wrote
;;; `(n ?x :where 1..3)`, and the range, which S1f.5.3 retires, is not
;;; written here), filed `open` and owned by S1f.5.11, on the user's ruling
;;; of 2026-11-07 (Q-M1e2.28).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `p` facts and a rule whose premise writes the pair `:k v` inside the
;;; pattern `(p ?x …)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A term takes no keyword (Q-M1e2.28), so the program is refused at load,
;;; with a line naming the rule and the clause: every command that reads it
;;; exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The compiler drops the pair, so `r` fires for every `p`: root derives
;;; `(q a)` and `(q b)`, with no line (`ein saturate FILE --dump`). The pair's
;;; value `v` is still a name of the program, so `(p v)`, `(q v)` and the
;;; `hit` facts over `a`, `b` and `v` are candidates, and R is 24 states, 32
;;; commitments, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at
;;; `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s, release and debug alike).

(relation p Node) (relation q Node) (relation hit Node)
(p a) (p b)
(rule r () :match (p ?x :k v) :assert (q ?x))
